-- title:
--   Invariance of Pic⁰ torsion under constant field extension
-- statement:
--   Let $K \subseteq K'$ be fields with $K$ algebraically closed of characteristic $0$ and $K'$ algebraically closed, and let $F/K$, $F'/K'$ be field extensions fitting into compatible towers $K \to K' \to F'$ and $K \to F \to F'$ (the two scalar-tower hypotheses). Assume $F$ contains an element transcendental over $K$ over which $F$ is finite, and likewise for $F'$ over $K'$; assume further that $F$ and $F'$ satisfy `IsCurveOver` over $K$ and $K'$ respectively, that is: every nonzero element of the field has an associated finitely supported divisor of degree zero recording its orders at all places, every place has residue field finite-dimensional over the constant field, and the module of Kähler differentials is free of rank one over the field. Assume also that $K'$ together with the image of $F$ in $F'$ generates $F'$, i.e. the intermediate field of $F'/K'$ generated by the range of $F \to F'$ is all of $F'$. Here a place of $F/K$ is a proper valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$ which is a principal ideal ring, a divisor is a finitely supported $\mathbb{Z}$-valued function on places, and $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ is the quotient of the kernel of the degree map by the subgroup of divisors of the form $v \mapsto v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ for some nonzero $f$ in the field. Then for every nonzero natural number $n$, the $n$-torsion subgroups (the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodules annihilated by $n$) of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F'/K')$ and of $\mathrm{Pic}^0(F/K)$ have the same cardinality as natural numbers.
--
--   This is the rigidity of Jacobian torsion under extension of an algebraically closed base field of characteristic zero: the $n$-torsion of the degree-zero divisor class group does not grow when the constant field is enlarged. It is used in the finiteness of the torsion of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, in the construction of an injective conorm map on torsion, and in the computation of the order of the $p$-torsion as a power determined by the genus, where it permits a reduction to the case of the complex numbers.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Pic0_natCard_torsion_eq_of_constantFieldExtension.lean

import Mathlib
